Counting in bases

The binary number system just like the usual base
ten that we are used to is another way of
counting.

The binary number system have just two digits
(0,1) which is called bit.

The highest digit in any base is equal to the base
minus one. For example counting from {0 - 9}in

base ten, though there are ten digits, the highest
digit is 9. This apply to all other bases.

For base two (binary) there are two digits i.e.
(0,1)



Counting in binary

¢ Counting in binary system is just as is done in
base ten which we are very familiar with.

e When we count from 0 — 9 in base ten, the next
digit after 9 is 10 (one, zero) what we call ten.
When we count from 11 to 19, the next digit
becomes 20

¢ Since binary has just two digits, when we count
from 0-1, the next digit becomes 10 (one, zero)
just as we did same in base ten when the highest
digit is reached.



